      "id": "a1-u01-d06-t01",
      "before": "a1-u01-d06-e01",
      "title": { "en": "ź and dź" },
      "body": { "en": "Ź is the soft partner of ż, as ś is of sz: źle. Dź is like a soft English j, as in \"jeans\". Before a vowel they are spelt zi and dzi, which is why dzień, dziękuję and do widzenia sound the way they do." }
    },
    {
      "id": "a1-u01-d06-t02",
      "before": "a1-u01-d06-e03",
      "title": { "en": "ń, dz and dż" },
      "body": { "en": "Ń is like the ny in \"canyon\", and ni before a vowel is the same sound: nie, nieźle. Dz is like the ds in \"kids\": bardzo. Dż is like the j in \"jam\", and the Polish word dżem means just that." }
    }
  ],
